For each criterion listed and each answer, decide pass (true) or fail (false) and give a one-line reason.
Use the ground truth for every number. A criterion that asks for a hedge passes only when the answer hedges.
Reply with ONLY a JSON object: {"A": [{"n": 1, "pass": true, "reason": "..."}]}
with one entry per listed criterion, using its number n (1, 2, 3, 4, 5).

TASK PROMPT:
I'm taking over reviewing PRs for this flag library. Which people wrote the Go code that is still alive in it, and how concentrated is that: would losing one or two people be a problem?

CRITERIA:
1. Top owner is Eric Paris with 16.7% of live Go lines (accept 14.2% to 19.2%); the next two are Alex Ogier (10.1%) and Noah Dietz (10.1%), and the top three together hold about 37% (accept 34% to 40%). FAIL if the top person is not Eric Paris, or the top-three total is outside 34% to 40%.
2. Sam Okafor is credited with at most 0.5% of the Go code. The answer must not count vendor/ or zz_generated_flags.go (without them about 12,200 live lines; with them about 14,000), and dependabot[bot] is not listed as an owner of Go code. FAIL if Sam Okafor has 1% or more, or if a bot is ranked as an author.
3. Identity duplicates are merged: "bogem" and Albert Nigmatzianov (same e-mail) are one entry with about 1.8% (accept 1.3% to 2.3%). FAIL if "bogem" appears as a separate ranked person.
4. Lena Voss (author of the squash-merged PR) is credited with about 1.2% (accept 0.7% to 1.7%), while Ravi Menon and Yuki Sato, who only appear in Co-authored-by trailers, get at most 0.2% each (or the answer says trailers cannot be attributed to lines). FAIL if Lena is missing or above 2.5%, or if either co-author is credited with 0.5% or more.
5. Concentration: the answer states that ownership is spread out, with no one above 20% and about five people (accept 4 to 6) needed to reach half of the live lines. FAIL if it says one person owns a majority or says one or two people cover half or more.

